WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.517
Labor-market data: young workers first.

2
00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:06.445
The Stanford Digital Economy Lab’s “Canaries in the Coal Mine?

3
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:11.824
” (Brynjolfsson, Chandar, and Chen) uses large-scale ADP payroll data.

4
00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:32.520
Its August 2026 update found no widespread job displacement from AI, but a widening gap for young workers: employment of 22 to 25 year olds in the most AI-exposed occupations was about 19% below where it would have been had it kept pace with peers in less exposed occupations,

5
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.656
up from the gap in the first version.

6
00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:47.501
The adjustment ran mainly through reduced hiring rather than layoffs, and in occupations where AI is used to complement workers, employment was flat or rising, especially for experienced workers.

7
00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:54.331
The authors report that interest rates, remote work, and the tech boom and bust do not appear to explain the pattern.

8
00:00:54.611 --> 00:01:12.680
Narayanan and Kapoor’s June 2026 essay reads the same period from the software side: tasks can be automated without jobs disappearing, because deciding what to build and verifying what ships remain human bottlenecks, and US software employment was still growing,

9
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.688
though more slowly than it might have.

10
00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:19.235
Put together, the 2026 evidence fits neither extreme.

11
00:01:19.515 --> 00:01:30.715
It supports “AI is changing who gets hired, starting at the entry level, and productivity gains are real but uneven,” and does not yet support “jobs are disappearing broadly.

12
00:01:30.995 --> 00:01:31.292
”.
